(Plan 310 task 4 - the instance possessive. `origin's magnitude squared` is
 sugar for `'magnitude squared' of origin`: the receiver fills the function's
 first parameter and any remaining arguments follow the call preposition
 (§4). It is a compile-time rewrite into an ordinary call, so everything the
 rewritten form does - copying a thing argument, returning a thing, being
 inferred into a declaration - is task 3's machinery unchanged.

 A field always wins: `origin's x` stays a field read even though a function
 could be written with the same name, because the collision rule refuses that
 program at the function's own line rather than letting one shadow the other.

 Acceptance: the six lines in the .expected, in that order - the free call and
 the sugared call agreeing, a call that carries a further argument, the sugar
 read from a quoted variable name, and the sugar read through a field that
 holds a thing.)

A thing called point has
  a number called x is 0,
  a number called y is 0.

A thing called segment has
  a point called start,
  a point called end.

(The magnitude itself needs a square root, so what this returns is the
 squared form - and that is what its name says.)
To 'magnitude squared' with a point called corner.
  a number called 'x squared' is corner's x multiply corner's x.
  a number called 'y squared' is corner's y multiply corner's y.
  Return a number, 'x squared' add 'y squared'.

To 'scaled by' with a point called corner and a number called factor.
  a point called scaled.
  Set scaled's x to corner's x multiply factor.
  Set scaled's y to corner's y multiply factor.
  Return a point, scaled.

a point called origin.
Set origin's x to 3.
Set origin's y to 4.
Print 'magnitude squared' of origin.
Print origin's 'magnitude squared'.
Print origin's x.

The 'tripled corner' is origin's 'scaled by' on 3.
Print 'tripled corner''s x.

a point called 'the point in question'.
Set 'the point in question''s x to 7.
Print 'the point in question''s 'magnitude squared'.

(A receiver is anything that names a whole thing, so a field holding one
 reads the same way.)
a segment called 'the line'.
Set 'the line''s end's y to 12.
Print 'the line''s end's 'magnitude squared'.
